      <span>/</span>
      <span class="text-emerald-400">Philippines 13th Month Pay</span>
    </nav>

    <!-- EXACTLY ONE H1 -->
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl md:text-5xl font-black tracking-tight text-white leading-tight mb-6">
      Philippines 13th Month Pay & Mandatory SSS/PhilHealth Costs Guide
    </h1>

    <!-- 45-60 Word Featured Snippet Box with Green Accent Border -->
    <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-slate-900 via-slate-900/90 to-slate-950 border-2 border-emerald-500 rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-xl shadow-emerald-500/10 mb-10">
      <div class="flex items-center space-x-2.5 text-xs font-bold font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-emerald-400 mb-2">
        <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-emerald-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z" />
        </svg>
        <span>Quick Answer: Philippines 13th Month Pay & Employer Burden</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm md:text-base text-slate-200 leading-relaxed font-medium">
        In the Philippines, employers must pay statutory 13th month pay by December 24 to all rank-and-file employees completing at least one month of service. Calculated as Total Basic Salary Earned During Calendar Year / 12, it is augmented by mandatory employer contributions: SSS pension funds, PhilHealth medical coverage (5% premium split equally), and Pag-IBIG housing fund (HDMF).
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Main Article Body -->
    <div class="prose prose-invert max-w-none space-y-10 text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed">
      
      <!-- Section 1: The 13th Month Pay Legal Framework -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">1.</span> The Statutory 13th Month Pay Mandate (PD 851)
        </h2>
        <p>
          Established under <strong>Presidential Decree No. 851</strong>, 13th month pay is a mandatory benefit required of all employers in the Philippines. It is not a performance bonus; it is a legally enforceable right for all rank-and-file employees who have worked for at least one (1) month during the calendar year, regardless of designation, employment status, or pay frequency.
        </p>
        <p>
          The statutory payment deadline is strict: employers must disburse this amount <strong>on or before December 24</strong> of each year. Employers commonly split payments into two tranches—one in May/June (for school enrollment) and the remainder in December—but the full balance must be settled before Christmas Eve.
        </p>

        <!-- Formula Highlight Box -->
        <div class="bg-slate-950 border border-emerald-500/40 rounded-xl p-6 my-6 text-center">
          <span class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-emerald-400 font-mono font-bold block mb-2">Statutory Legal Formula</span>
          <div class="text-lg sm:text-2xl font-black text-white font-mono bg-slate-900 py-3 px-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-800 inline-block">
            13th Month Pay = Total basic salary earned during calendar year / 12
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 mt-3 max-w-xl mx-auto">
            Where "basic salary" includes all remunerations or earnings paid by an employer to an employee for services done, but excludes monetary benefits not considered part of base compensation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white mb-2">What Is Included vs Excluded in "Basic Salary":</h3>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-4">
          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-800">
            <h4 class="text-emerald-400 font-bold text-xs uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 font-mono">&#10003; Included in Calculation</h4>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-4 text-xs space-y-1 text-slate-300">
              <li>Regular base monthly salary</li>
              <li>Paid sick leaves (SL) taken</li>
              <li>Paid vacation leaves (VL) taken</li>
              <li>Maternity/paternity paid base allowances</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-800">
            <h4 class="text-rose-400 font-bold text-xs uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 font-mono">&#10007; Excluded from Calculation</h4>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-4 text-xs space-y-1 text-slate-300">
              <li>Overtime pay & night shift differential</li>
              <li>Premium pay for regular/special holidays</li>
              <li>Unused leave cash conversions</li>
              <li>Discretionary performance bonuses & commissions</li>
              <li>Cost of living allowances (COLA) & transportation perks</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-slate-900/40 border-l-4 border-cyan-400 p-4 rounded-r-xl text-xs text-slate-300">
          <strong class="text-cyan-400 font-mono">Tax Exemption Threshold (TRAIN Law):</strong> Under Republic Act No. 10963, 13th month pay and other de minimis bonuses are completely exempt from Philippine personal income withholding tax up to <strong>PHP 90,000</strong>. Any amount exceeding PHP 90,000 is added to taxable compensation income.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: Statutory Employer On-Costs (SSS, PhilHealth, Pag-IBIG) -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">2.</span> Mandatory Statutory Employer On-Costs
        </h2>
        <p>
          Foreign tech companies hiring in the Philippines via an Employer of Record (EOR) or direct entity must budget for three statutory social contribution funds in addition to the 13th month pay:
        </p>

        <!-- Statutory Table -->
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-950 my-6 shadow-xl">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-xs text-slate-300">
            <thead class="bg-slate-900 text-slate-200 font-mono uppercase text-[11px] border-b border-slate-800">
              <tr>
                <th class="py-3.5 px-4 font-bold">Statutory Fund</th>
                <th class="py-3.5 px-4 font-bold text-emerald-400">Employer Share</th>
                <th class="py-3.5 px-4 font-bold text-slate-400">Employee Share</th>
                <th class="py-3.5 px-4 font-bold text-cyan-400">Monthly Salary Ceiling</th>
                <th class="py-3.5 px-4 font-bold text-amber-400">Max Monthly Employer Cost</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-800/70 font-sans">
              <tr class="hover:bg-slate-900/40">
                <td class="py-3.5 px-4 font-bold text-white">Social Security System (SSS)</td>
                <td class="py-3.5 px-4 font-mono text-emerald-400">9.5% + EC</td>
                <td class="py-3.5 px-4 font-mono text-slate-400">4.5%</td>
                <td class="py-3.5 px-4 font-mono">PHP 30,000 (MSC)</td>
                <td class="py-3.5 px-4 font-mono text-amber-300">PHP 2,880 + PHP 30 (EC) = PHP 2,910</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="hover:bg-slate-900/40">
                <td class="py-3.5 px-4 font-bold text-white">PhilHealth (Health Insurance)</td>
                <td class="py-3.5 px-4 font-mono text-emerald-400">2.5%</td>
                <td class="py-3.5 px-4 font-mono text-slate-400">2.5%</td>
                <td class="py-3.5 px-4 font-mono">PHP 100,000</td>
                <td class="py-3.5 px-4 font-mono text-amber-300">PHP 2,500 (flat cap)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="hover:bg-slate-900/40">
                <td class="py-3.5 px-4 font-bold text-white">Pag-IBIG Fund (HDMF)</td>
                <td class="py-3.5 px-4 font-mono text-emerald-400">2.0%</td>
                <td class="py-3.5 px-4 font-mono text-slate-400">2.0%</td>
                <td class="py-3.5 px-4 font-mono">PHP 10,000 (statutory cap)</td>
                <td class="py-3.5 px-4 font-mono text-amber-300">PHP 200 (mandatory)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-900/80 font-bold border-t border-slate-700">
                <td class="py-3.5 px-4 text-emerald-400">Total Mandatory On-Costs</td>
                <td class="py-3.5 px-4 font-mono text-emerald-400">~14.0% + EC</td>
                <td class="py-3.5 px-4 font-mono text-slate-400">~9.0%</td>
                <td class="py-3.5 px-4 text-slate-300">Capped across all tiers</td>
                <td class="py-3.5 px-4 font-mono text-emerald-300">PHP 5,610 / month maximum</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-3 text-xs text-slate-300">
          <p>
            <strong>SSS (Social Security System):</strong> Covers sickness, retirement, disability, and death benefits. Regular SSS applies to salaries up to PHP 20,000 Monthly Salary Credit (MSC), while earnings between PHP 20,000 and PHP 30,000 flow into the mandatory Workers' Investment and Savings Program (WISP). In addition, the employer pays an Employees' Compensation (EC) fund contribution of PHP 10 or PHP 30 depending on MSC.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>PhilHealth:</strong> Under the Universal Health Care (UHC) Law, the premium rate is 5.0% of basic monthly income, equally divided between employer (2.5%) and employee (2.5%). It has a statutory floor of PHP 10,000 (minimum PHP 250 ER share) and a ceiling of PHP 100,000 (maximum PHP 2,500 ER share).
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Pag-IBIG (HDMF):</strong> Provides national housing loans and provident savings. While voluntary contributions can be higher, the mandatory statutory ceiling is pegged at a maximum monthly salary of PHP 10,000, capping mandatory employer contributions at exactly PHP 200 per month.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: Concrete Cost Model Example -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">3.</span> True Cost of Hiring: Senior Software Engineer Example
        </h2>
        <p>
          Let's model the true all-in monthly and annual employer expense for hiring a Senior Full-Stack Engineer in Manila earning a competitive gross base salary of <strong>PHP 150,000 / month</strong> (~$2,650 USD):
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-950 my-6 shadow-xl">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-xs font-mono text-slate-300">
            <thead class="bg-slate-900 text-slate-200 uppercase text-[11px] border-b border-slate-800">
              <tr>
                <th class="py-3 px-4">Expense Component</th>
                <th class="py-3 px-4">Monthly Cost (PHP)</th>
                <th class="py-3 px-4">Annual Cost (PHP)</th>
                <th class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-400">% of Base Salary</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-800/70">
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold text-white">Gross Base Salary</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4">PHP 150,000</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4">PHP 1,800,000</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-slate-400">100.00%</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-slate-300">13th Month Pay (Annual Accrual / 12)</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-400">PHP 12,500</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-400">PHP 150,000</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-400">8.33%</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-slate-300">SSS Employer Contribution (Max Cap + EC)</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4">PHP 2,910</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4">PHP 34,920</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-slate-400">1.94%</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-slate-300">PhilHealth Employer Contribution (Max Cap)</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4">PHP 2,500</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4">PHP 30,000</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-slate-400">1.67%</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-slate-300">Pag-IBIG Employer Contribution (Max Cap)</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4">PHP 200</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4">PHP 2,400</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-slate-400">0.13%</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-slate-900/80 font-bold border-t border-slate-700 text-white">
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-400">Total Statutory Employer Cost</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-300">PHP 168,110 / mo</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-300">PHP 2,017,320 / yr</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-300">112.07% (12.07% on-cost)</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5 text-xs text-slate-300">
          <strong class="text-white">Key Takeaway for Hiring Managers:</strong> Unlike European jurisdictions where employer payroll taxes add 25% to 42% on top of base compensation, the Philippines features statutory caps on SSS, PhilHealth, and Pag-IBIG. For software engineers earning PHP 100,000 to PHP 250,000+, total statutory on-costs (including 13th month pay) range between <strong>11% and 14%</strong>, making the Philippines one of the most cost-efficient technical talent hubs in Asia.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4: Compliance Pitfalls & Contractor Misclassification -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">4.</span> Philippine Labor Law & Contractor Misclassification
        </h2>
        <p>
          Many international companies attempt to bypass 13th month pay, SSS, and PhilHealth contributions by classifying Filipino staff as independent contractors under B2B service contracts paid via Wise or Payoneer.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the Philippines, the Department of Labor and Employment (DOLE) and the Supreme Court apply the strict <strong>"Four-Fold Test"</strong> to determine employer-employee relationships:
        </p>
        <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 text-xs text-slate-300 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Selection and Engagement:</strong> Did the company directly interview and hire the worker?</li>
          <li><strong>Payment of Wages:</strong> Does the worker receive periodic fixed monthly compensation rather than deliverable-based milestone invoices?</li>
          <li><strong>Power of Dismissal:</strong> Can the company terminate the worker for cause or performance?</li>
          <li><strong>Power of Control (The Decisive Test):</strong> Does the company control not just what work is done, but the means, methods, tools, and schedule used to accomplish it?</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          If control exists, Philippine labor tribunals deem the worker a regular employee. Retroactive liabilities include back-pay for all unpaid 13th month bonuses, retroactive SSS/PhilHealth contributions with 24% annual penalty surcharges, and statutory security of tenure protections preventing at-will termination.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

